00; 457*38=17366, ≡34. no (need 33!). Close but no.\n\n461: 10^4: 461*21=9681, ≡319≡-142. 10^8≡20164; 461*43=19823, ≡341. 10^10≡34100; 461*73=33653, ≡447. no.\n\n463: 10^4: 463*21=9723, ≡277. 10^8≡277^2=76729; 463*165=76395, ≡334. 10^10≡33400; 463*72=33336, ≡64. no.\n\n467: 10^4: 467*21=9807, ≡193. 10^8≡193^2=37249; 467*79=36893, ≡356. 10^10≡35600; 467*76=35492, ≡108. no.\n\n479: 10^4: 479*20=9580, ≡420≡-59. 10^8≡3481; 479*7=3353, ≡128. 10^10≡12800; 479*26=12454, ≡346. no.\n\n487: 10^4: 487*20=9740, ≡260. 10^8≡260^2=67600; 487*138=67206, ≡394. 10^10≡39400; 487*80=38960, ≡440. no.\n\n491: 10^4: 491*20=9820, ≡180. 10^8≡32400; 491*65=31915, ≡485≡-6. 10^10≡-600; -600+491*2=382. no.\n\n499: 10^4: 499*20=9980, ≡20. 10^8≡400. 10^10≡40000; 499*80=39920, ≡80. no.\n\n503: 10^4: 503*19=9557, ≡443≡-60. 10^8≡3600; 3600-3521(503*7)=79. 10^10≡7900; 503*15=7545, ≡355. no.\n\nGood—so no prime ≤ 503 divides 9999999967. Hence no factor ≤ 503 divides, so no recursion; loop runs factors 2..503, count reaches 502 at factor=503, before checking divisibility. Output JSON:\n\njson.dumps({'factor': 503, 'limit': 99999, 'text': '9999999967', 'value': 9999999967}, ensure_ascii=False, sort_keys=True, separators=(',', ':'), allow_nan=False)\n\nSorted keys: factor, limit, text, value.\n\nSo: {\"factor\":503,\"limit\":99999,\"text\":\"9999999967\",\"value\":9999999967}\n\nThen '\\n' appended, and SystemExit raised, so print never executes.\n\nWait—double check the limit: math.isqrt(9999999967). 99999^2 = 9999800001. 100000^2 = 10000000000 > 9999999967. So isqrt = 99999. Yes.\n\nAlso double check count logic: for factor in range(2, limit+1). First iteration factor=2, count becomes 1. So count==502 at factor = 2 + 501 = 503. Yes.\n\nBut wait—also verify build is called only once (no earlier recursion). Also check the initial palindrome/usable check: text \"9999999967\" — usable (no '0')? digits: 9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,6,7 — yes no zero. Palindrome? \"9999999967\" vs reverse \"7699999999\" — not palindrome. So loop.\n\nHmm, but careful: the check `if value % factor or not usable(factor)` — even if some factor were usable, no factor divides value as established. Also count increments happen before the divisibility check, so it doesn't matter.\n\nThus output line: {\"factor\":503,\"limit\":99999,\"text\":\"9999999967\",\"value\":9999999967}\n\nLet me double check a few of my modular computations to be safe, particularly 457 (gave 34) — that's not 33, fine. Also check some primes where I might have made errors but got non-33; the only risk is if actual remainder is 33 somewhere. Let me verify a couple tricky ones with direct division: check 9999999967 / 7: 7*1428571421 = 9999999947, remainder 20. Not divisible. Good. 11: alternating sum: digits from right: 7,6,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9 → odd positions (from right, positions 1,3,5,7,9): 7+9+9+9+9=43; even positions: 6+9+9+9+9=42; difference 1, not divisible by 11. Good.\n\nCheck 13: 10^10 ≡ 3 mod 13 computed; verify: 10^3=1000≡1000-1001=-1 mod 13 (13*77=1001). So 10^3≡-1, 10^9≡-1, 10^10≡-10≡3. Yes. 33 mod 13=7. Not equal.\n\nCheck 37: 999=27*37, so 10^3≡1, 10^9≡1, 10^10≡10. 33≠10. Good.\n\nCheck 457: 10^4 mod 457: 457*21=9597, 10000-9597=403. 403^2=162409. 162409 mod 457: 457*355=162235, 162409-162235=174. So 10^8≡174. 10^10=174*403=70122. 457*153=69921, 70122-69921=201? Hmm that contradicts my earlier computation of 34. Let me redo.\n\nEarlier I said 10^4 ≡ -54 (i.e., 403 ≡ -54, correct since 457-54=403). 10^8 ≡ (-54)^2 = 2916. 2916 - 457*6=2742, ≡174. Then 10^10 ≡ 174 * (-54) = -9396. 457*20=9140, -9396+9140=-256, +457=201. Hmm, I previously wrote \"10^10≡17400; 457*38=17366, ≡34\" — that was wrong: 10^10 = 10^8 * 10^2? No wait, 10^10 = 10^8 * 10^2, not 10^8 * 10^4! Let me recheck all computations: 10^10 = 10^8 * 10^2. Oh no, in many places I used 10^8 * 10^4? Let me check: I defined 10^4 then 10^8, then 10^10 = 10^8 * 10^2. In the 457 case I multiplied 174*100 = 17400, which is 10^8*10^2 = correct. But my redo above multiplied 10^8 * 10^4 — that's 10^12, wrong. So 10^10 ≡ 174*100 = 17400 mod 457: 457*38 = 17366, 17400-17366 = 34. So 34. OK earlier was right. Good.\n\nLet me re-verify a few others where I might have multiplied wrong, but the pattern 10^10 = 10^8*100 was used consistently. Spot-check some:\n\n- 7: 10^8 mod 7: 10^6≡1 so 10^8≡10^2≡2. 2*100=200; 200 mod 7 = 200-196=4. 33 mod 7=5. 4≠5 ✓.\n\n- 13: 10^8: 10^6≡1 → 10^8≡100≡9. 9*100=900 mod 13: 13*69=897, ≡3. 33 mod 13 = 7. ✓ (matches earlier).\n\n- 17: 10^8≡-1 (from 10^4≡4, 10^8≡16≡-1). -1*100=-100 ≡ -100+102=2. 33 mod 17=16. ✓.\n\n- 271: 10^4≡-27, 10^8≡729≡729-542=187. 187*100=18700; 271*69=18699, ≡1. So 10^10≡1 mod 271; need 33; 271>33 so no. ✓.\n\n- 331: 10^8≡266, *100=26600; 331*80=26480, ≡120. ✓ no.\n\nAlso verify 457 isn't a divisor: 34 ≠ 33 ✓.\n\nNow also double-check the reading: `n = int(sys.stdin.buffer.readline())` with input \"9999999967\" → 9999999967. Yes.\n\nAlso note: JSON key sort: 'factor','limit','text','value' — alphabetical order: factor < limit < text < value ✓.\n\njson.dumps with separators (',',':') gives no spaces.\n\nSo output:\n{\"factor\":503,\"limit\":99999,\"text\":\"9999999967\",\"value\":9999999967}\n\nOne more consideration: Does `print(build(n) or -1)` happen before? No, SystemExit raised in build.\n\nAlso verify count starts at 0 and reaches exactly 502 (never skips). Yes.\n\nThus final answer.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"{\"output\":\"{\\\"factor\\\":503,\\\"limit\\\":99999,\\\"text\\\":\\\"9999999967\\\",\\\"value\\\":9999999967}\\n\"}"}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"zai-coding-cn","model":"glm-5.3","usage":{"input":361,"output":10101,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":10067,"totalTokens":10462,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1787641914764,"responseId":"202608251511558c297cf80792484f","rawStopReason":"stop"}}
